    "created_at": "2026-04-06T19:20:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-06T19:20:00Z",
    "body": "In chromium, we have added features to devtools to let authors know they did something wrong in this case in addition to others when the content model is not followed.\n\nI think that adding buttons with secondary actions inside options was a followup plan at some point, although I haven't seen any discussions about this in a while.\n\n> Stripping the interactive elements during parsing\n\nWhen we changed the html parser to stop dropping tags inside the select element, it was a very big breaking change and I'd prefer not to make additional parser changes. This also won't help with authors who use frameworks like react to create their DOM because that doesn't necessarily use the HTML parser.\n\n> Rendering the elements visually, but forcing them to be inert.\n\nI suppose that we could force all child nodes of option elements to be inert by using the controversial interactivity property like this:\n```css\noption > * {\n  interactivity: inert !important;\n}\n```\nI imagine this would have the effect of pushing authors away from using customizable select, since hopefully our devtools signaling is enough to already let them know that they are going to run into a11y issues and they should reconsider what they're doing. If we ever figure out how to make secondary actions work, this could also preclude us from making it polyfillable.\n\nIf we did something like this to option elements, should we do the same thing to every other interactive element in the browser, like button elements?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
